  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 222 PM EST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nVisible satellite imagery reveals an incoming deck of clouds from \nour southwest. A mid/upper level trough will dig in over the Ohio \nriver valley tonight with the surface low tracking to our north. The \nattached cold front will move through tonight into Thursday bringing \nscattered rain showers through the region. Forecast soundings \nindicate sufficient instabilty and steep enough lapse rates to \nsupport a few rumbles of thunder, but severe weather is not expected \ngiven the lack of meaningful instabilty. On Thursday, much colder \nair will begin to filter into the Tennessee Valley region. Our upper \nlevel low closes off over the Great Lakes region on \nThursday/Thursday night and will act as the center of a pinwheel for \nmultiple weak shortwave disturbances to pivot around in the cyclonic \nflow. The first will move through Thursday evening into Thursday \nnight and bring additional surge of cold air and an increase in \nPoPs. Additional disturbances will move through the area through \nSaturday with subtle uptick in precipitation, especially in the \neastern Tennessee Mountains. \n\nIf we take a look aloft, h850 temperatures will begin to fall \nThursday evening as one of the shortwaves move through. Forecast \nsoundings support h850 temperatures going below 0c with values \nbetween -4 to -6c Thursday night and Friday. This combined with the \nstrong northwest h850 flow will help changeover any rain showers to \nsnow showers in the higher elevations and the mountains. While \nforecast soundings are not fully saturated through the DGZ, the \ncombination of favorable flow off the western Great Lakes and \nupslope flow should encourage snow showers throughout Friday in the \nmountains. Elsewhere, isolated to scattered rain showers are likely \nwith a non-zero chance of a few wet flakes mixing in at times. \n\nSo how much snow will fall in the mountains? Despite being 48 hours, \nthere remains enough spread in guidance that continues to paint an \nuncertain picture. The GFS suite has continued to trend downward \nwhile the ECMWF suite remains steady. The 12z RRFS nearly double \nsnowfall in Smokies compared to it's 06 and 00z runs. Our forecast \nis generally a minor snow event with totals of 1 to 3\" and a few 4-\n5\" spots in the mountain peaks. This will be the first legitimate \nmeasurable snow in the mountains this \"cold\" season. Impacts from \nthis early season winter weather will be minor and tied to the \nmountains. Anyone traveling through the mountains Friday/Saturday \nshould exercise caution if it's snowing. Other impacts should be \nminor given the limited scope of this event. An elevation based \nadvisory for our mountains may be needed, but there is some \nuncertainty present we have opted to wait until there is greater \nforecast confidence.\n\nI would not be surprised to see locally higher amounts possible if \nthe Euro-based solutions play out. EPS has 80%+ probabilities of 3\" \nor more in the mountains and only 40 to 50% of 6\" in the highest \npeaks. This is a higher end scenario, but it isn't too far fetched \nand is worth watching. The 24 to 36 hour stretch of favorable low-\nmid level flow off Lake Michigan tied with upsloping should \ntranslate to a good bit of snow. The previous weeks have been mild \nand dry with our soil temperatures elevated for this time of year. \nThat will likely limit initial accumulations. TLDR: I wouldn't be \nsurprised if the highest elevations managed a little more snow than \nthe current forecast has.\n\nPrecipitation tapers off on Saturday as the upper low moves off the \ncoast and the northwest flow weakens. Behind the upper level low, a \nhigh pressure builds in across the TN-OH Valley region that will \nbring dry conditions for early next week. Temperatures will \ngradually warm into early next week. Values remaining below normal \nuntil mid-week.",
